Clearing tracks means removing or hiding evidence of activities performed on a system so that attackers (or testers in labs) are not easily detected.
In ethical hacking, this is studied to understand attacker behavior and improve defenses.

⚠️ Important: This module is theoretical + defensive awareness.
Never perform this on real systems without explicit authorization.

1. Why Clearing Tracks Matters

Attackers clear tracks to:

Avoid detection

Maintain persistence

Delay incident response

Hide identity & activity

Defenders study this to:

Detect intrusions

Improve logging

Strengthen monitoring

2. Types of Tracks Left Behind

During an attack, traces are left in:

Log files

Command history

Authentication records

Network traffic

Temporary files

Registry (Windows)

Memory (RAM)

