4. Common Clearing Track Techniques (Conceptual)
1️⃣ Log Manipulation

Deleting logs

Modifying log entries

Replacing logs with fake data

⚠️ Deleting logs is very suspicious and easy to detect.

2️⃣ Command History Removal

Shell history records commands used.

Linux:

.bash_history

.zsh_history

Indicators:

Missing or truncated history files

Sudden resets

3️⃣ File Timestamp Manipulation

Files have:

Created

Modified

Accessed times

Attackers may try to alter timestamps to blend in.

4️⃣ Temporary File Cleanup

/tmp

/var/tmp

Browser cache

App temp files

5. Windows Track Artifacts
Windows Stores Evidence In

Event Viewer

Registry

Prefetch files

Recent files

Recycle Bin

Indicators of tampering:

Disabled logging

Cleared event logs

Missing prefetch files

6. Network-Level Tracks

Even if logs are deleted locally:

Firewalls

IDS/IPS

Routers

Proxy logs

💡 Network logs are harder to erase.

7. Advanced Track Hiding (High-Level)

(For awareness only)

Living-off-the-land (LOLBins)

In-memory execution

Encrypted communication

Log flooding

These techniques are studied to detect, not perform.

8. Detection of Track Clearing

Blue teams look for:

Gaps in logs

Log size anomalies

Time mismatches

Missing history files

Unusual admin activity

9. Clearing Tracks vs Anti-Forensics
Clearing Tracks	Anti-Forensics
Remove evidence	Obfuscate evidence
Simple deletion	Encryption & hiding
Easier to detect	Harder to detect
10. Defensive Countermeasures

🔐 Centralized logging (SIEM)
🔐 Log integrity monitoring
🔐 File integrity monitoring
🔐 Immutable logs
🔐 Regular audits

